WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — interface-economics (90s) Narrator Edwin

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:07.619
First, single handoff failures carry exact price tags, like losing a Mars mission over mismatched units.

2
00:00:08.119 --> 00:00:15.988
Second, giant national software-failure numbers come from broad surveys and models, never counting handoffs alone.

3
00:00:16.488 --> 00:00:23.450
Third, checking what each side assumes is cheap, but one team pays while everyone else benefits.

4
00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:28.253
On Ariane 5's first flight, guidance software reused from Ariane 4

5
00:00:28.253 --> 00:00:33.663
assumed a smaller horizontal velocity than the new rocket produced, destroying four

6
00:00:33.663 --> 00:00:40.834
science satellites and forcing a two-hundred-fourteen-million European-currency recovery mission to fly again.

7
00:00:41.334 --> 00:00:47.601
Industry presentations routinely mash audited accident budgets together with executive opinion polls

8
00:00:47.601 --> 00:00:53.681
into fake multi-trillion-dollar totals. Our ledger keeps exact accounted losses strictly separate

9
00:00:53.681 --> 00:01:03.458
from fifty-nine-point-five-billion-dollar testing models, eighty-five-billion-dollar developer time surveys, and trillion-dollar software quality estimates.

10
00:01:03.958 --> 00:01:10.648
Standards economics explains why fragile handoffs persist year after year: a checked interface is

11
00:01:10.648 --> 00:01:16.373
a public good with private upfront costs. One upstream team pays sprint hours today

12
00:01:16.373 --> 00:01:22.719
to specify exact units and bounds, while every downstream user collects the avoided outages.

13
00:01:23.219 --> 00:01:29.423
When shared machine-checked representations carry units, versions, and operating ranges automatically

14
00:01:29.423 --> 00:01:33.415
by construction, the private cost to any single team of closing a

15
00:01:33.415 --> 00:01:37.837
software handoff drops toward the tiny cost of running the check itself.
